            <ArticleTitle>The value of magnetic resonance imaging in investigating a painful total knee arthroplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>977.e9-977.e12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arth.2010.07.028</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This case describes a rare cause of pain around a total knee arthroplasty and highlights the importance of a structured approach to the investigation of these patients. Magnetic resonance imaging provided detailed information about the cause of pain despite the presence of a prosthetic joint, and the pathology, primary non-Hodgkin lymphoma, was treated without the need for further surgical intervention.</AbstractText>
